# Development happens on Windows; the deployment target is a Linux container.
# LF everywhere: in the repository AND in the working tree on every platform.
#
# "text=auto" alone would only normalise the stored blobs, leaving Windows
# checkouts as CRLF and the two platforms disagreeing on disk. The explicit
# "eol=lf" also fixes the working tree, and takes precedence over a machine's
# core.autocrlf — which matters because Git for Windows sets autocrlf=true in
# its system config by default.
#
# This file is the enforcement point rather than git config precisely because it
# is committed: it applies to every clone on every machine, whereas core.autocrlf
# is per-machine and never travels with the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The exception. cmd.exe parses batch files byte-by-byte and can mis-handle
# labels and multi-line constructs in an LF-only file, so these must stay CRLF.
# PowerShell has no such problem and is left as LF with everything else.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# Better hunk headers in diffs. Text handling itself is already covered above.
*.cs            diff=csharp
*.razor         diff=html
*.css           diff=css
*.md            diff=markdown

# Binary — never diff or normalise
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.webp          binary
*.db            binary
*.snk           binary
